hematics/volume-56/issue-1/MONOTONICITY-OF-RESISTANCE-DISTANCE-IN-LINEAR-2-TREES/10.1216/rmj.2026.56.123.full\n\nzbMATH Keywords\n\nmonotonicityresistance distanceeffective resistance2-tree\n\nMathematics Subject Classification ID\n\nApplications of graph theory (05C90) Trees (05C05) Extremal problems in graph theory (05C35) Planar graphs; geometric and topological aspects of graph theory (05C10) Distance in graphs (05C12) Applications of graph theory to circuits and networks (94C15)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResistance distance in straight linear 2-trees (https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.05883)\nciteturn3academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.9 years ago; Francis ... Our result also gives formulae for the number of spanning trees and 2-forests in a straight linear 2-tree.\nTitle: Resistance distance in straight linear 2-trees\nAuthors: Wayne Barrett, Emily J. Evans, Amanda E. Francis\nDate: Sat Dec 16 00:46:23 2017\n\nWe consider the graph $G_n$ with vertex set $V(G_n) = \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$ and $\\{i,j\\} \\in E(G_n)$ if and only if $0<|i-j| \\leq 2$. We call $G_n$ the straight linear 2-tree on $n$ vertices. Using $\\Delta$--Y transformations and identities for the Fibonacci and Lucas numbers we obtain explicit formulae for the resistance distance $r_{G_n}(i,j)$ between any two vertices $i$ and $j$ of $G_n$. To our knowledge $\\{G_n\\}_{n=3}^\\infty$ is the first nontrivial family with diameter going to $\\infty$ for which all resistance distances have been explicitly calculated. Our result also gives formulae for the number of spanning trees and 2-forests in a straight linear 2-tree. We show that the maximal resistance distance in $G_n$ occurs between vertices 1 and $n$ and the minimal resistance distance occurs between vertices $n/2$ and $n/2+1$ for $n$ even (with a similar result for $n$ odd). It follows that $r_n(1,n) \\to \\infty$ as $n \\to \\infty$. Moreover, our explicit formula makes it possible to order the non-edges of $G_n$ exactly according to resistance distance, and this ordering agrees with the intuitive notion of distance on a graph. Consequently, $G_n$ is a geometric graph with entirely different properties than the random geometric graphs investigated in [6]. These results for straight linear 2-trees along with an example of a bent linear 2-tree and empirical results for additional graph classes convincingly demonstrate that resistance distance should not be discounted as a viable method for link prediction in geometric graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n/usr/uumath/bin/dvialw       -outfile:/u/ftp/pub/tex/bib/linala2020.ps linala2020.dvi (https://ftp.math.utah.edu/pub/tex/bib/linala2020.pdf)\nciteturn3search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear 2-trees.\n*Linear Algebra and its Applications*, 584(??):107–126, January 1, 2020. CODEN LAAPAW. ISSN 0024-3795 (print), 1873-1856 (electronic). URL http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0024379519303933.\n\n[BEFSC22]  \n**Bolten:2022:TMS**  \nMatthias Bolten, Sven-Erik Ekström, Isabella Furci, and Stefano Serra-Capizzano. Toeplitz momentary symbols: definition, results, and limitations in the spectral analysis of structured matrices. *Linear Algebra and its Applications*.\n\n[BE20]  \n1873-1856 (electronic). URL http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0024379524000612.\n\n[BEFSC22]  \n*Linear Algebra and its Applications*, 606(??):41–67, December 1, 2020. CODEN LAAPAW. ISSN 0024-3795 (print), 1873-1856 (electronic). URL http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0024379520303220.\n\n[BEFSC22]  \n**Barrett:2020:RDS**  \nWayne Barrett, Emily J. Evans, and Amanda E. Francis. Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear 2-trees. *Linear Algebra and its Applications*, 606(??):41–67, December 1, 2020. CODEN LAAPAW. ISSN 0024-3795 (print), 1873-1856 (