. We also give alternative and perhaps more transparent versions of these formulas in terms of block matrices. As an application we find the resistance distance between the extremal vertices of the “corrugated linear 2-tree” and use this result to determine its asymptotic rate of growth as the number of vertices goes to infinity. Finally we note that our formulas give a different way to determine all the resistance distances in a “fan graph.”\n\nKeyword(s)\n5C12 , 05C50 , 15A15 , 15B48 , 94C15 , Effective resistance , Resistance distance , 2u2013tree , Spanning 2u2013forest\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear 2-trees (2020) | Wayne Barrett | 12 Citations (https://scispace.com/papers/resistance-distance-and-spanning-2-forest-matrices-of-linear-1ntsgkl2eb)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear 2-trees ... PDF\n\nJournal Article10.1016/J.LAA.2020.06.031\n\n# Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear 2-trees\n\nWayne Barrett,Emily J. Evans,Amanda Francis +2 more\n\n- 01 Dec 2020\n\n - Linear Algebra and its Applications \n\n - Vol. 606, pp 41-67\n\nPDF\n\n12\n\nSave\n\nCite\n\nTL;DR: In this article, the authors generalize these formulas to linear 2-trees with any number of bends and give alternative and perhaps more transparent versions of these formulas in terms of block matrices.\n\nread more\n\nAbout: This article is published in Linear Algebra and its Applications. The article was published on 01 Dec 2020. The article focuses on the topics: Resistance distance.\n\nread more--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nE. J. Evans - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/E._J._Evans)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Publication  | Date of Publication  | Type ... Monotonicity of resistance distance in linear 2-trees ... Results from a differential equation model for cell motion with random switching show that the model cell velocity is asymptotically independent of force ... Resistance distance in bent linear 2-trees  | 2017-12-15  | Paper\n# E. J. Evans\n\nFrom MaRDI portal\n\nMaRDI QIDQ1732093zbMATH OpenFDO\n\nList of research outcomes\n\nThis list is not complete and representing at the moment only items from zbMATH Open and arXiv. We are working on additional sources - please check back here soon!\n\nPublication  | Date of Publication  | Type\n--- | --- | ---\nAn Introductory Survey of Recursions in the Computation of Resistance Distance\nThe Fibonacci Quarterly  | 2026-05-28  | Paper\nMonotonicity of resistance distance in linear 2-trees\nRocky Mountain Journal of Mathematics  | 2026-03-17  | Paper\nRecursions and characteristic polynomials of the rows of the circuit array\nThe Fibonacci Quarterly  | 2024-11-28  | Paper\nSteady state distributions of moving particles in one dimension: with an eye towards axonal transport\nJournal of Mathematical Biology  | 2024-11-07  | Paper\nRecursions and characteristic polynomials of the Rows of the Circuit Array  | 2023-05-21  | Paper\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMonotonicity of Resistance Distance in Linear 2-Trees (https://eurekamag.com/research/104/981/104981431.php)\nciteturn3search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # Monotonicity of Resistance Distance in Linear 2-Trees ... 2019: Resistance distance-based graph invariants and the number of spanning trees of linear crossed octagonal graphs Journal of Applied Mathematics and Computing 63(1-2): 1-27 ... 1999: Maximal monotonicity of dense type, local maximal monotonicity, and monotonicity of the conjugate are all the same for continuous linear operators Pacific Journal of Mathematics 189(1): 1-20\n\n# Monotonicity of Resistance Distance in Linear 2-Trees\n\n##### Evans, E.J.; Francis, A.E.\n\n* * *\n\n#### Rocky Mountain Journal of Mathematics 56(1): 123-135\n\n#### 2026\n\n* * *\n\nISSN/ISBN: 0035-7596\n\nAccession: 104981431\n\n##### Full-Text Article emailed within 1 workday\n\n##### Summary\n\nRecall that a linear 2-tree, sometimes called a 2-path, is a 2-tree with exactly two vertices of degree two. In this article we will address two open questions regarding resistance distance in linear 2-trees. The first question is: given an arbitrary linear 2-tree does resistance distance between two vertices u, v increase as |v-u| increases? We answer this question in the affirmative. As a corollary to this result, we show that the maximal resistance distance in a linear 2-tree occurs between the vertices of degree 2 (the extremal vertices). The second question concerns the optimal location of bends in a linear 2-tree. We show that for a linear 2-tree with a single bend, the location of the bend that minimizes the maximal resistance distance (i.e., the resistance distance between the degree 2-vertices) is as close as possible to a degree 2 vertex. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRECURSIONS AND CHARACTERISTIC POLYNOMIALS OF THE ROWS (https://www.fq.math.ca/Papers/62-4/hendel08232024rev2.pdf)\nciteturn3search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; This paper has explored the polynomial sequences of CX, which is a polynomial general- ... Francis, Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear ... Discrete Applied Mathematics, 320 (2022), 387–407.\nTHE FIBONACCI QUARTERLY\n5. Conclusion\nThis paper has explored the polynomial sequences of CX, which is a polynomial general-\nization of the numerical circuit array that presents equivalent resistance values occurring in\nthe central regions of the collection of n-grids and their reductions. Interesting simply stated\npatterns emerge, some of which we have proved. We believe this to be a fertile ground for\nfuture research.\nReferences\n[1] R. B. Bapat and S. Gupta, Resistance distance in wheels and fans, Indian J. Pure App. Math., 41.1\n(Feb. 2010), 1–13.\n[2] W. Barrett, E. J. Evans, and A. E. Francis, Resistance distance in straight linear 2-trees, Discrete\nAppl. Math., 258 (2019), 13–34.\n[3] W. Barrett, E. J. Evans, and A. E. Francis, Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear\n2-trees, Linear Algebra Appl., 606 (2020), 41–67.\n[4] G. Boole, A Treatise on the Calculus of Finite Differences, 2nd ed., Dover, Mineola, New York, 2014.\n[5] D. DeTemple and W. Webb, Combinatorial Reasoning: An Introduction to the Art of Counting, Wiley,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAN INFINITE 2-DIMENSIONAL ARRAY ASSOCIATED WITH ELECTRIC (https://www.fq.math.ca/Papers1/60-5/evans.pdf)\nciteturn3search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Francis, Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices ... Spanning 2-\nassures us that the other arguments are functions of the leftmost elements of previous rows,\ne < E. This completes the proof.\nReferences\n[1] Wayne Barrett, Emily J. Evans, and Amanda E. Francis. Resistance distance in straight linear 2-trees.\nDiscrete Appl. Math. 258 (2019), 13–34. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2018.10.043\n[2] Wayne Barrett, Emily J. Evans, and Amanda E. Francis, Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices\nof linear 2-trees. Linear Algebra Appl. 606 (2020), 41–67. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.laa.2020.06.031\n[3] Wayne Barrett, Emily J. Evans, Amanda E. Francis, Mark Kempton, and John Sinkovic. Spanning 2-\nforests and resistance distance in 2-connected graphs, Discrete Appl. Math. 284 (2020), 341–352. https:\n//www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X20301608\n[4] E. J. Evans and A. E. Francis, Algorithmic techniques for ﬁnding resistance distances on structured\ngraphs, Discrete Appl. Math. 320 (2022), 387–407. https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/\npii/S0166218X22001329\n[5] E. J. Evans and R. J. Hendel, Resistance values under transformations in regular triangular grids, https:\n//doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2207.11207.\n[6] R. J. Hendel, Limiting Behavior of Resistances in Triangular Graphs, https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.\n01959.\n[7] Thomas Koshy, Fibonacci and Lucas numbers with applications, New York, NY: Wiley-Interscience, 2001.\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/9781118033067\n[8] Mathworld, Stirling’s Approximation. https://mathworld.wolfram.com/StirlingsApproximation.html\n[9] Neil J. A. Sloane and The OEIS Foundation Inc. OEIS: The on-line encyclopedia of integer sequences.\nhttp://oeis.org/\nMSC2020: 11B39, 33C05\nBrigham Young University\nEmail address: EJEvans@math.byu.edu\nTowson University\nEmail address: RHendel@Towson.Edu\nDECEMBER 2022\n171--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResistance distance in bent linear 2-trees (https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.05859)\nciteturn3academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.9 years ago; Francis ... We call the graph $G_n$ with vertex set $V(G_n) = \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$ and $\\{i,j\\} \\in E(G_n)$ if and only if $0<|i-j| \\leq 2$ a straight linear 2-tree.\nTitle: Resistance distance in bent linear 2-trees\nAuthors: Wayne Barrett, Emily J. Evans, Amanda E. Francis\nDate: Fri Dec 15 22:11:45 2017\n\nIn this note we consider the bent linear 2-tree and provide an explicit formula for the resistance distance $r_{G_n}(1,n)$ between the first and last vertices of the graph. We call the graph $G_n$ with vertex set $V(G_n) = \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, n\\}$ and $\\{i,j\\} \\in E(G_n)$ if and only if $0<|i-j| \\leq 2$ a straight linear 2-tree. We define the graph $H_n$ with $V(H_n)= V(G_n)$ and $E(H_n) = (E(G_n) \\cup \\{k,k+3\\})\\setminus(\\{k+1,k+3\\}$ to be a bent linear 2-tree with bend at vertex $k$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nResistance distance and Kirchhoff index in hexagonal subdivision of graphs (https://ouci.dntb.gov.ua/en/works/4Krro0wJ/)\nciteturn3search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Barrett W, Evans EJ, Francis AE (2020) Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear 2-trees. ... Chen B, Huang J (2022) On unitary Cayley graphs of matrix rings. ... Hong Y, Zhu Z, Luo A (2019) Extremal graphs with diameter 2 for two indices on resistance-distance.\n\n# Resistance distance and Kirchhoff index in hexagonal subdivision of graphs\n\n## General information\n\nPublication type\n\nJournal Article\n\nDOI\n\n10.1007/s40314-025-03281-0\n\nJournal\n\n2025, Computational and Applied Mathematics, № 6\n\nPublisher\n\nSpringer Science and Business Media LLC\n\nAuthors\n\nYujun Yang, Can Xu, Wensheng Sun\n\n## Details\n\nNumber of citations\n\n2\n\nNumber of works in the list of references\n\n30\n\nIndexed in Scopus\n\nYes\n\nIndexed in Web of Science\n\nYes\n\n## List of references\n\n  1. Azimi A, Bapat RB, Farrokhi DGM (2021) Resistance distance of blowups of trees. Discrete Math 344(7):11238\nDOI: 10.1016/j.disc.2021.112387\n  2. Balakrishnan R, Krishnamoorthy S, So W (2023) Resistance distance in connected balanced digraphs. Discrete Appl Math 337:46–53\nDOI: 10.1016/j.dam.2023.04.014\n  3. Barrett W, Evans EJ, Francis AE (2020) Resistance distance and spanning 2-forest matrices of linear 2-trees. Linear Algebra Appl 606:41–67\nDOI: 10.1016/j.laa.2020.06.031\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the resistance distance and Kirchhoff index of a linear hexagonal (cylinder) chain - Science Explorer Abstract (https://scixplorer.org/abs/2020PhyA..55824999H/abstract)\nciteturn3search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## On the resistance distance and Kirchhoff index of a linear hexagonal (cylinder) chain ... The monotonicity and some asymptotic properties of resistance distances in L_{n} and R_{n} are given.\n\n## On the resistance distance and Kirchhoff index of a linear hexagonal (cylinder) chain\n\n### Authors\n\nHuang, Sumin;\n\nLi, Shuchao\n\nRefereed Article\n\nFull Text Sources\n\nOther Resources\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe resistance between two nodes in some resistor networks has been studied extensively by mathematicians and physicists. Let L_{n} be a linear hexagonal chain with n 6-cycles. Then identifying the opposite lateral edges of L_{n} in an ordered way yields the linear hexagonal cylinder chain, written as R_{n}. We obtain explicit formulae for the resistance distance r_{L_{n}}(i , j) (resp. r_{R_{n}}(i , j)) between any two vertices i and j of L_{n} (resp. R_{n}). One may see that {L_{n} }_{n=1}^{∞} and {R_{n}}_{n=1}^{∞} are two nontrivial families with diameter going to ∞ for which all resistance distances have been explicitly calculated. We determine the maximum and the minimum resistance distances in L_{n} (resp. R_{n}). The monotonicity and some asymptotic properties of resistance distances in L_{n} and R_{n} are given. As well we give formulae for the Kirchhoff indices of L_{n} and R_{n} respectively.\n\n### Details\n\nPublication  | Physica A: Statistical Mechanics and its Applications, Volume 558, article id. 124999.\nPublication Date  | November 2020\nDOI  | 10.1016/j.physa.2020.124999 10.48550/arXiv.1905.09017\narXiv  | arXiv:1905.09017\nBibcode  | 2020PhyA..55824999H\nCollections  | physics general\nKeywords  | 05C50 Mathematics - Combinatorics\nE-Print Comment(s)  | 20pages, 15 figures,; doi:10.1016/j.physa.2020.124999--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA recursion formula for resistance distances and its applications - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/A_recursion_formula_for_resistance_distances_and_its_applications)\nciteturn3search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; Kirchhoff index resistance distance recursion formula ...   * The matrix-forest theorem and measuring relations in small social gr